---
title: Performance
description: Where the allocation savings come from, the benchmark numbers, and the shape where Ergosfare does not win.
---

The design premise is that all dispatch-shape work happens at compile time or once per
message type, never per dispatch. This page shows what that buys and what it does not.

## Where the work goes

**Compile time.** The source generator pre-computes handler descriptors and emits dispatch
roots that close the runtime's generic executor and invoker types over each message. Nothing
has to be discovered or constructed at startup.

**Once per `(message type, group set)`.** The pipeline plan is built and cached
process-wide: six fixed stages, each a pre-ordered array, with covariant matching resolved
and generic handler types already closed. Group filters and `[ExcludeFromPipeline]` are
applied here, once.

**Every dispatch.** Rent a pooled context, look up the cached executor for the message's
runtime type, walk the plan, resolve handler *instances* from the calling scope, return the
context. No reflection, no `MakeGenericType`, no registry scan, no `AsyncLocal`.

## Pooled contexts

Execution contexts are rented from a `[ThreadStatic]`-first pool — a plain load and store on
the common same-thread cycle — and returned on completion. A synchronous fast path skips the
async state machine entirely when the whole pipeline completes synchronously.

This is the single largest contributor to the allocation numbers below, and it is the source
of the one rule the API imposes: a context is valid only for the duration of its dispatch.
See [Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/execution-context#pooling-and-the-lifetime-rule).

## Typed all the way down

Executors and invokers are closed over each message's concrete runtime type, so handlers are
invoked through their typed members with no object-typed bridge anywhere. `MakeGenericType`
survives only as a fallback for open generics and runtime-only registrations.

## Benchmarks

BenchmarkDotNet with `[MemoryDiagnoser]`. Each operation performs **100 000 sequential
dispatches** of a no-op handler.

Environment: BenchmarkDotNet v0.15.8, Windows 11, AMD Ryzen 7 7800X3D, .NET 9.0.11
(RyuJIT x86-64-v4). Measured on the `preview` branch, 2026-07-24.

Two shapes are measured, for three mediators:

- **Typical usage** — how an application normally sends messages: the library's public
  mediator interface, one shared DI scope for the whole loop.
- **Web-server shape** — a fresh DI scope for every dispatch, mimicking one scope per HTTP
  request, which is the shape ASP.NET Core gives you.

| Scenario (100k dispatches/op) | Mean | Allocated | Gen0/1k ops |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| **Ergosfare** — typical usage | 6.87 ms | **2.29 MB** | 47 |
| **MediatR** — typical usage | 6.09 ms | 18.31 MB | 375 |
| **LiteBus** — typical usage | 146.96 ms | 714.87 MB | 14 750 |
| **Ergosfare** — web-server shape | 18.81 ms | 38.91 MB | 813 |
| **MediatR** — web-server shape | 10.09 ms | 33.57 MB | 688 |
| Ergosfare — internal engine path (reference row) | 6.49 ms | 5.34 MB | 109 |

Reproduce with:

```bash
dotnet run -c Release -f net9.0 --project test/Stella.Ergosfare.Benchmarking
```

## Reading the table honestly

**The typical-usage rows are the headline.** At comparable latency, Ergosfare allocates about
an eighth of MediatR's garbage, with Gen0 collection pressure down accordingly. That is the
effect of pooled contexts and the `ValueTask`-first pipeline.

**MediatR wins the web-server shape**, on both time and allocation. When every dispatch gets
a fresh DI scope, scope creation dominates and Ergosfare's per-dispatch savings are swamped
by resolving handlers from a cold scope. If your workload is one dispatch per HTTP request,
this row is the one that describes you — and the difference is not large in either direction
next to the cost of the request itself.

**The internal engine path is a reference row, not a recommendation.** It drives
`IMessageMediator` directly with pre-built options, bypassing the public facade and its pooled
context. It exists to show the facade adds no hidden cost. You would not write application
code this way.

**LiteBus is a reference point, not a target.** Ergosfare's API surface was heavily inspired
by it, but the runtime is an independent implementation.

**Transient handlers allocate on purpose.** One instance per dispatch is what a transient
registration declares. Dispatch-heavy single-scope loops that want instance reuse should
register handlers as singletons or call `ForceMemoizedHandlers()`.

## Row to method mapping

For matching against your own runs:

| Table row | BenchmarkDotNet method |
|---|---|
| Ergosfare — typical usage | `StellaErgosfare_PublicApi` |
| MediatR — typical usage | `MediatR` |
| LiteBus — typical usage | `LiteBus_PublicApi` |
| Ergosfare — web-server shape | `StellaErgosfare_PublicApi_ScopePerDispatch` |
| MediatR — web-server shape | `MediatR_ScopePerDispatch` |
| Ergosfare — internal engine path | `StellaErgosfare` |

## Getting the most out of it

- Use [`RegisterGenerated()`](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/compile-time-discovery). Runtime
  scanning costs startup time and forfeits the AOT guarantees.
- Return `ValueTask` without `async` when the handler completes synchronously.
- Register stateless handlers as singletons.
- Keep interceptors off high-volume messages with
  [`[ExcludeFromPipeline]`](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights).
- Measure your own workload. These numbers are a no-op handler in a tight loop; yours is not.

Next: [Native AOT and trimming](/ergosfare.docs/advanced/aot-and-trimming).
